  ('86806277-3870-5e86-80eb-87e58aa9b38c', 'ea65b685-d0c0-5aa8-bb8d-d6bb01f68d36', 'Memory and addressing modes (b)', 'Memory addressing in the machine code is performed by the instructions that contain opcode and operand.

Note: opcode ADD adds the operand next to it to the value in the accumulator
operand A is the cell A in the memory (i.e. a variable saved under this name)
operand Index is the value in the Index register to be added to the direct address

Fill in the table below using the letters (A–I) with appropriate definitions and examples of the memory addressing modes. There may be more than one letter in a cell.

A The operand gives the address that stores the value to be used by the instruction

B The operand gives the actual value to be used by the instruction

C ADD A+Index

D ADD A

E The address of the operand is determined by adding the operand to the value stored in the index register.

F ADD #10

G ADD (A)

H ADD 10

I The operand gives the address of a register that stores another address, where the value is located', 'table_completion', 9, 'exam', 'complete', 'HYBRID', 12, '60897a48-c2f2-526e-b15e-c52852e09a07', 'e4c53106-09e0-5ad8-9665-876f37a5fc01', '{"columns":["Memory addressing mode","Definition","Example"],"rows":4,"prefill":[["immediate addressing",null,null],["direct addressing",null,null],["indirect addressing",null,null],["indexed addressing",null,null]]}'::jsonb, 'past_paper', 'NIS 2024 Paper 1 Q6(b)', false, 'published', 'e58a618f-e21c-58c2-be36-bef718c60322', '(b)', 1),
