  ('9d27e211-cd3c-5dfa-849f-76fdaeb92c16', 'b4e198a7-34b1-5851-92ea-d98f741f91ce', 1, true, 'Any one (1): stages are completed one after another in a fixed order / sequential; each stage must be finished (signed off) before the next begins; it is difficult to go back to an earlier stage; documentation is produced at the end of each stage; testing takes place after coding', '[{"criterion":"Valid feature","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Each stage is completed and signed off before the next stage begins.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('6329b3ff-f026-55a2-be45-5644a9ae3343', '355af6bd-281d-5ca8-b8b4-1998b9144f83', 1, true, 'For each reason: identification (1) + explanation linked to the scenario (1). Max 2 reasons.
- Requirements are fixed by law (1) so they can be fully defined at the start and will not need to change later (1)
- Documentation is produced at every stage (1) which meets the agency''s requirement for complete documentation (1)
- Clear stages and milestones (1) so the agency can monitor progress / cost and time can be estimated (1)', '[{"criterion":"Reason 1 identified","marks":1},{"criterion":"Reason 1 explained using the scenario","marks":1},{"criterion":"Reason 2 identified","marks":1},{"criterion":"Reason 2 explained using the scenario","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 The tax rules are fixed by law, so all requirements can be written at the start and the inflexibility of waterfall is not a problem. 2 Waterfall produces documentation at the end of every stage, which meets the agency''s demand for complete documentation.', '{}'::jsonb, 'The explanation mark needs a link to the scenario (fixed law, documentation, monitoring). Do not credit the same reason twice.', null),
  ('dcdb5041-4f3d-587e-ab6d-2ac4a2be879f', 'be57aeeb-edbc-5216-b21b-2f65deb458f4', 1, true, 'Any two (1 each): inflexible — changes are difficult / expensive once a stage is finished; the client sees the working system only at the end; errors are found late (testing after coding); little user involvement during development; not suitable when requirements are unclear or likely to change; long projects may no longer meet users'' needs when finished', '[{"criterion":"First disadvantage","marks":1},{"criterion":"Second, different disadvantage","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 Changes are hard to make once a stage has been signed off. 2 The client only sees a working system at the end.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('63abe0db-0902-57da-a6fd-db3488ecfb70', '88bcf9a1-808a-550d-b844-c6f1b25313aa', 1, true, 'Testing (stage) (1) — accept: during testing near the end of the project / acceptance testing', '[{"criterion":"Testing","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The testing stage.', '{"values":["testing","the testing stage","testing stage"]}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('30bcc38e-6f81-56d0-a4b5-10333be70f4f', '3c441481-cf05-5578-9630-ea4996fc882c', 1, true, '- The team plans / selects features (user stories) from the backlog for the sprint (1)
- designs, codes and tests these features (1)
- produces a working part (increment) of the app (1)
- which is reviewed by / shown to the client or users for feedback (1)

Max 2', '[{"criterion":"Features are planned / chosen for the sprint","marks":1},{"criterion":"Features are built and tested, producing a working increment reviewed by the client","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The team chooses a few features from the backlog, designs, codes and tests them, and at the end shows the working part of the app to the investors for feedback.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('5b78b112-6bfb-553d-ba13-789ea1826f85', '6272d68e-a326-5298-aa50-c3febde655e8', 1, true, 'For each advantage: identification (1) + analysis of its effect in the scenario (1). Max 2 advantages.
- Working software is delivered early / after every sprint (1) so investors can see progress quickly / the app can reach users sooner (1)
- Requirements can change between sprints (1) so features can be adapted to users'' feedback (1)
- The client / users are involved throughout (1) so the final app better meets their needs (1)
- Problems are found early in each sprint (1) so they are cheaper to fix (1)', '[{"criterion":"Advantage 1 identified","marks":1},{"criterion":"Advantage 1 linked to the start-up","marks":1},{"criterion":"Advantage 2 identified","marks":1},{"criterion":"Advantage 2 linked to the start-up","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 A working version is produced after every two-week sprint, so investors can see the app early, as they want. 2 Requirements can be changed between sprints, so new features can be added or changed after users give feedback.', '{}'::jsonb, 'The second mark of each advantage needs a link to the start-up scenario (investors, user feedback). Do not credit the same advantage twice.', null),
  ('80e128d1-3963-52aa-805a-7aaabd72048c', '5c1c324f-48d7-5c9c-bd7c-adb0d26e8d66', 1, true, 'One disadvantage (1) + explanation (1):
- final cost / completion date are hard to predict (1) because requirements keep changing / the number of sprints is not fixed (1)
- investors must attend reviews regularly (1) which takes a lot of their time (1)
- less documentation (1) so later maintenance may be harder / more expensive (1)
- scope may keep growing (1) increasing cost (1)', '[{"criterion":"Identifies a disadvantage","marks":1},{"criterion":"Explains its effect on the investors","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The total cost and finishing date are hard to predict, because features keep changing after each sprint, so investors cannot be sure how much money they will need.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('a561b7a3-3e3d-5aa2-adf4-157c022aae6d', '347ea515-17fd-585d-8237-135962393b0b', 1, true, 'Client involvement (1): agile — client involved throughout / reviews every sprint; waterfall — mainly at the start and the end

When working software is delivered (1): agile — after every sprint (early and often); waterfall — only at the end of the project', '[{"criterion":"Correct difference in client involvement","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correct difference in delivery of working software","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Client involvement: in agile the client reviews every sprint, in waterfall mainly at the start and end. Delivery: agile delivers working software every sprint, waterfall only at the end.', '{}'::jsonb, 'Each answer must refer to both models (or clearly contrast them).', null),
  ('ce310fe3-9d1a-51e9-b3e5-7c989ab621f3', 'b4e58142-7500-592b-9b25-64796fe25577', 1, true, 'One mark per phase described:
1 Determine objectives / requirements / alternatives for this loop (1)
2 Identify and resolve risks — risk analysis, often building a prototype (1)
3 Develop and test the next version / engineering (1)
4 Evaluate (client review) and plan the next iteration / loop (1)', '[{"criterion":"Phase 1: objectives / requirements","marks":1},{"criterion":"Phase 2: risk analysis / prototype","marks":1},{"criterion":"Phase 3: develop and test","marks":1},{"criterion":"Phase 4: evaluate and plan the next loop","marks":1}]'::jsonb, '1 Determine the objectives of the loop. 2 Analyse the risks and reduce them, e.g. with a prototype. 3 Develop and test the next version. 4 The client evaluates it and the next loop is planned.', '{}'::jsonb, 'Accept the phases in any wording; one mark per correct phase even if not in order.', null),
  ('b8205876-0eac-5455-88e6-b5a133896726', '62832b2e-6543-5875-9cf2-b87ea875e14b', 1, true, '- The project is high-risk / large / expensive (1)
- the spiral model analyses and reduces risks in every loop (e.g. with prototypes) before the full system is built (1)
- security problems can be found early (1)

Max 2', '[{"criterion":"Refers to the high risk / size of the project","marks":1},{"criterion":"Spiral analyses and reduces risks in every loop","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'The bank''s project is large and high-risk, and the spiral model carries out a risk analysis in every loop, so security and technical risks are found and reduced with prototypes before the whole system is built.', '{}'::jsonb, null, null),
  ('bd074fce-48f4-5360-b337-01880a7cecba', 'a952cdd5-9753-5384-b524-33c40fef9a6c', 1, true, 'Analysis of models (max 4):
- Waterfall: clear structure, documentation, cost estimated (1); but inflexible, risks and errors found late — dangerous for a security-critical system (1)
- Agile: flexible, early working versions, client feedback (1); but less documentation, unpredictable cost, risk management not central — weak for strict security rules (1)
- Spiral: risk analysis every loop, prototypes reduce risk, suitable for large projects (1); but expensive, needs risk specialists, timescale uncertain (1)

Conclusion (max 2):
- Spiral is most suitable (1)
- justified by the scenario: high risk / security / large and expensive project, so the cost of risk analysis is worth it (1)

Accept another conclusion only if well justified from the scenario.', '[{"criterion":"Advantage of a first model","marks":1},{"criterion":"Disadvantage of the first model","marks":1},{"criterion":"Advantage of a second model","marks":1},{"criterion":"Disadvantage of the second model","marks":1},{"criterion":"Clear conclusion naming the most suitable model","marks":1},{"criterion":"Conclusion justified using the scenario","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'Waterfall is easy to manage and well documented, but it is inflexible and testing happens late, so security problems might only be found at the end. Agile delivers working versions early and adapts to change, but it has less documentation and less control of cost and risk. The spiral model analyses risks in every loop and uses prototypes, which suits a large, high-risk system, although it is expensive and needs specialists. The spiral model is the most suitable, because the bank''s project is large, expensive and security-critical, so the extra cost of risk analysis is justified.', '{}'::jsonb, 'Analysis marks need specific advantages/disadvantages, not just names. Maximum 4 analysis marks and 2 conclusion marks. The justification must use the scenario (risk, security, size, cost).', null),
  ('d929a200-1a86-5007-800c-d91f4401ef05', '3699b89b-a2cc-59c1-8a6e-eb45278408d2', 1, true, 'B: `Low ← Middle + 1` (1)', '[{"criterion":"Selects the correct update for searching the upper half of the array.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'B', '{"correct":"B"}'::jsonb, '', 'If the target is greater than the middle value in an ascending array, all values at or below `Middle` can be discarded. The next search interval starts at `Middle + 1`.'),
  ('48355574-6fc8-5125-86d0-c2ad58151281', '4fe91eab-1187-5521-9117-39a740ddd139', 1, true, 'False (1)', '[{"criterion":"States that the statement is false.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'False', '{"correct":false}'::jsonb, '', 'Binary search depends on comparing the target with the middle value and discarding half of the remaining data. This only works if the data is sorted into a known order.'),
  ('503aeefa-f7a4-594c-a60a-d6ed4993fd32', '33b06714-168d-5edb-872b-d416a61fe699', 1, true, 'L1 matched to R1 (1)
L2 matched to R2 (1)
L3 matched to R3 (1)
L4 matched to R4 (1)', '[{"criterion":"Correctly matches bubble sort to adjacent comparisons and swaps.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correctly matches insertion sort to inserting into a sorted section.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correctly matches binary search to middle item and halving.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Correctly matches linear search to sequential checking.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'L1-R1, L2-R2, L3-R3, L4-R4', '{"pairs":{"L1":"R1","L2":"R2","L3":"R3","L4":"R4"}}'::jsonb, '', 'Bubble sort works by adjacent swaps. Insertion sort places the current item into the already sorted part of the list, while binary search repeatedly halves a sorted search space.'),
  ('fa249aa5-a569-545d-8758-ef9b20138330', '5ef7d358-dea2-5ce8-ad1c-6419eadaf322', 1, true, '`N - 1` for the outer loop limit (1)
`N - Pass` for the inner loop limit (1)
`>` as the comparison for ascending order (1)

Blank (2): accept N - 1 (correct but checks the already-sorted end of the array).', '[{"criterion":"Uses `N - 1` as the final outer pass.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Uses `N - Pass` as the final adjacent comparison position.","marks":1},{"criterion":"Uses `>` so larger values are swapped towards the end.","marks":1}]'::jsonb, 'FOR Pass ← 1 TO N - 1
